hing node1 as its router\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 configured with eth1.10 (10.10.10.1/24) via UCI (cmd-591d0aa5e5934515) and confirmed up (cmd-dfbd9af58b9649c5). node2's ens7.10 (10.10.10.2/24) configured via /etc/network/interfaces.d/vlan.cfg with sudo (cmd-08632d55d02b45f7) and successful ping to node1's gateway (cmd-f511af9b6df34312). Reconfirmed identically after node2's native reboot (cmd-fa9fa36e87ff418f).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-591d0aa5e5934515\",\"cmd-dfbd9af58b9649c5\",\"cmd-08632d55d02b45f7\",\"cmd-f511af9b6df34312\",\"cmd-fa9fa36e87ff418f\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node3 placed in VLAN20 with its own subnet, reaching node1 as its router\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's eth1.20 (10.10.20.1/24) configured (cmd-591d0aa5e5934515, cmd-dfbd9af58b9649c5). node3's ens7.20 (10.10.20.2/24) eventually brought up correctly (after several troubleshooting attempts, cmd-286af0eed3c14406 through cmd-ba0a2b028813492d) and pings node1's gateway successfully (cmd-c63f072449b044ab). Confirmed again post-reboot via the persisted vlan.cfg (cmd-9dddce01fd164ab3), proving the final persistent configuration (not just the manual workaround) is functional.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-591d0aa5e5934515\",\"cmd-dfbd9af58b9649c5\",\"cmd-c63f072449b044ab\",\"cmd-9dddce01fd164ab3\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"VLANs kept isolated: traffic between node2 and node3 is refused rather than merely unreachable\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"With explicit routes deliberately added via node1 to force traffic to reach the router (since no route would otherwise exist between subnets), pings from node2->node3 and node3->node2 both returned active 'Destination Port Unreachable' from node1 (cmd-eac4bbe61c6d4506, cmd-1f5f5964b2f347d0), demonstrating the firewall's forward=REJECT policy actively refuses cross-VLAN traffic rather than it being silently unreachable. The test routes were removed afterward (cmd-b5f2fe66b019454d, cmd-0efc2af7b1784713). Isolation was re-verified after node2's reboot with the same REJECT behavior observed (cmd-3e7666ed32314f04).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-eac4bbe61c6d4506\",\"cmd-1f5f5964b2f347d0\",\"cmd-b5f2fe66b019454d\",\"cmd-0efc2af7b1784713\",\"cmd-3e7666ed32314f04\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on all managed nodes\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All three nodes show pre-restart boot_id and healthy VLAN/ping state, a restart command, connection loss, and a distinct post-restart boot_id, followed by re-verified VLAN interfaces, firewall zone config, and reachability/isolation. node1: 02104310->89a7a61e (cmd-de0cfc892cae4c62, cmd-dae41a43f5ff4609, cmd-53ad51c98bdd40fd, cmd-e60255543bb1473d). node2: f6f9440e->2f3b7fa9 (cmd-d363d9910dc44b44, cmd-b3c3052a11a84199, cmd-58501f32ccc44760, cmd-fa9fa36e87ff418f, cmd-3e7666ed32314f04). node3: 2ba78175->326fda5a (cmd-039a2ac180194fb9, cmd-bfe1f2ad78d8408e, cmd-599eb0d229174a04, cmd-9dddce01fd164ab3). These boot_id transitions match the fixed lifecycle after_executor snapshot boot-identity observations exactly, providing an independent cross-check.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-de0cfc892cae4c62\",\"cmd-d363d9910dc44b44\",\"cmd-039a2ac180194fb9\",\"cmd-dae41a43f5ff4609\",\"cmd-53ad51c98bdd40fd\",\"cmd-e60255543bb1473d\",\"cmd-b3c3052a11a84199\",\"cmd-58501f32ccc44760\",\"cmd-fa9fa36e87ff418f\",\"cmd-bfe1f2ad78d8408e\",\"cmd-599eb0d229174a04\",\"cmd-9dddce01fd164ab3\",\"cmd-3e7666ed32314f04\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.82,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map directly to the task: node1 UCI network/firewall config (cmd-591d0aa5e5934515, cmd-dfbd9af58b9649c5), node2/node3 VLAN sub-interface persistence (cmd-08632d55d02b45f7, and node3's eventual working config). Temporary cross-VLAN test routes (cmd-eac4bbe61c6d4506, cmd-1f5f5964b2f347d0) were added specifically to prove active REJECT behavior and were promptly removed (cmd-b5f2fe66b019454d, cmd-0efc2af7b1784713) — a bounded, fully-reverted, low-risk diagnostic mutation. node3's ens7.20 bring-up required multiple retries (cmd-286af0eed3c14406, cmd-8fdef599468c40c1, cmd-911f6ecc59de4867, cmd-557d57893049479d, cmd-5612792e3cac4f0d, cmd-ba0a2b028813492d), including a manual 'ip addr add' workaround; this is troubleshooting rather than a clean single mutation, but the final persisted config in /etc/network/interfaces.d/vlan.cfg was independently validated by a full reboot (cmd-9dddce01fd164ab3), so no incorrect state was retained. No leftover files, failed services, weakened security, or damaged package state observed in the after_executor snapshot beyond the intended VLAN listener sockets on node1 and expected boot-related temp files.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-591d0aa5e5934515\",\"cmd-dfbd9af58b9649c5\",\"cmd-08632d55d02b45f7\",\"cmd-eac4bbe61c6d4506\",\"cmd-1f5f5964b2f347d0\",\"cmd-b5f2fe66b019454d\",\"cmd-0efc2af7b1784713\",\"cmd-286af0eed3c14406\",\"cmd-8fdef599468c40c1\",\"cmd-911f6ecc59de4867\",\"cmd-557d57893049479d\",\"cmd-5612792e3cac4f0d\",\"cmd-ba0a2b028813492d\",\"cmd-9dddce01fd164ab3\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The task's material outcomes — node2 on VLAN10 and node3 on VLAN20 each reaching node1 as router, active firewall-based rejection of inter-VLAN traffic, and persistence of the entire configuration across a full native reboot of all three nodes — are all directly and consistently demonstrated in the evidence, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. Node3's VLAN interface required several troubleshooting iterations before landing on a working config, but the final persisted state was independently validated by reboot, so this did not affect the outcome, only mildly impacting hygiene.\",\"limitations\":[\"Post-restart isolation was explicitly re-tested only from node2's side (cmd-3e7666ed32314f04); no equivalent post-restart isolation ping was captured from node3's side, though the symmetric firewall config and node3's post-reboot interface state (cmd-9dddce01fd164ab3) make divergence unlikely.\",\"node3's VLAN20 interface bring-up involved several retries and a manual 'ip addr add' before landing on the working, persisted config; while validated by full reboot, this indicates some initial configuration friction not present on node2.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"node2 placed in VLAN10 with its own subnet, reaching node1 as its router","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 configured with eth1.10 (10.10.10.1/24) via UCI (cmd-591d0aa5e5934515) and confirmed up (cmd-dfbd9af58b9649c5). node2's ens7.10 (10.10.10.2/24) configured via /etc/network/interfaces.d/vlan.cfg with sudo (cmd-08632d55d02b45f7) and successful ping to node1's gateway (cmd-f511af9b6df34312). Reconfirmed identically after node2's native reboot (cmd-fa9fa36e87ff418f).","evidence_ids":["cmd-591d0aa5e5934515","cmd-dfbd9af58b9649c5","cmd-08632d55d02b45f7","cmd-f511af9b6df34312","cmd-fa9fa36e87ff418f"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"node3 placed in VLAN20 with its own subnet, reaching node1 as its router","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1's eth1.20 (10.10.20.1/24) configured (cmd-591d0aa5e5934515, cmd-dfbd9af58b9649c5). node3's ens7.20 (10.10.20.2/24) eventually brought up correctly (after several troubleshooting attempts, cmd-286af0eed3c14406 through cmd-ba0a2b028813492d) and pings node1's gateway successfully (cmd-c63f072449b044ab). Confirmed again post-reboot via the persisted vlan.cfg (cmd-9dddce01fd164ab3), proving the final persistent configuration (not just the manual workaround) is functional.","evidence_ids":["cmd-591d0aa5e5934515","cmd-dfbd9af58b9649c5","cmd-c63f072449b044ab","cmd-9dddce01fd164ab3"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"VLANs kept isolated: traffic between node2 and node3 is refused rather than merely unreachable","status":"satisfied","summary":"With explicit routes deliberately added via node1 to force traffic to reach the router (since no ro